  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a human–water system evolves through four historical phases, from the 1960s to a possible future, using colored arrows to show positive, negative, uncertain, and external effects. Across the sequence, the system shifts from simple growth-driven expansion to more interconnected governance and then toward uncertain resilience.\n\nIn the earliest “supply expansion” phase, population, economy, and urbanization all push water utilization upward, which leads to engineering solutions for water supply. The emphasis is on meeting rising demand, while ecological components such as vegetation, sediment, discharge, and precipitation appear secondary.\n\nIn the later “resource restriction” phase, institutional and engineering responses become more prominent, and restoration projects begin interacting with vegetation, discharge, and sediment. The system looks more constrained, with water use linked more clearly to environmental feedbacks.\n\nThe “complex governance” phase shows the densest network. Efficiency joins institutional solutions and restoration efforts, while water utilization, discharge, vegetation, and sediment are tightly coupled, suggesting a more managed but more complicated balance between development and ecosystem processes.\n\nThe future “resilience loop” is more uncertain. Social and technical transition, climate change, institutional solutions, and restoration projects all influence discharge, sediment, vegetation, and water utilization, indicating a system where adaptation and uncertainty become central.",
